Think of SignalR exactly like any other outbound port in your hexagon—just a contract, not an implementation detail of your domain logic. 

1. **Domain layer only holds the interface**  
   ```csharp
   public interface IRealTimeNotifier
   {
     IObservable<HubConnectionState> ConnectionStateStream { get; }
     IObservable<MessageModel>       JobStream             { get; }
     Task ConnectAsync();
     Task DisconnectAsync();
   }
   ```  
   That interface lives alongside your other ports (e.g. cache loaders, repositories). Nothing in the domain knows it’s SignalR under the hood.

2. **Application layer wires ports to use-cases**  
   Your `ConnectToServerSignalRUseCase` implements `IRealTimeNotifier` (or depends on it) and orchestrates “cache warmed → connect → start streaming.” Use dependency-injection to hand the implementation into whoever needs it (SplashForm, other services).

3. **Infrastructure layer contains the SignalR adapter**  
   In a separate project/folder (e.g. Infrastructure/Realtime):  
   ```csharp
   public class SignalRNotifier : IRealTimeNotifier
   {
     private readonly HubConnection _hub;
     …  
     public IObservable<HubConnectionState> ConnectionStateStream => _hub.ReactiveStateStream();
     public IObservable<MessageModel>       JobStream             => _hub.ReactiveJobUpdates();
     public Task ConnectAsync()    => _hub.StartAsync();
     public Task DisconnectAsync() => _hub.StopAsync();
   }
   ```  
   All the SignalR bits (Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client, Serilog, Watchdog registrations) stay out of your domain.

4. **Watchdog stays a pure registry**  
   It hides behind the same `IRealTimeNotifier`:  
   ```csharp
   // in your composition root, after building SignalRNotifier:
   WatchdogObject.RegisterConnectionState(signalRNotifier.ConnectionStateStream);
   WatchdogObject.RegisterJobUpdates    (signalRNotifier.JobStream);
