For calculating values when entering the second cell, here's a solution using `CellValueChanged`:

```csharp
private void radGridView1_CellValueChanged(object sender, GridViewCellEventArgs e)
{
    if(e.Column.Name == "Column2")
    {
        this.radGridView1.EndEdit();
        var row = e.Row as GridViewDataRowInfo;
        if(row != null)
        {
            // Calculate and assign to the rest of the row
            row.Cells["Col3"].Value = Compute(...);
            var bs = radGridView1.MasterTemplate.DataSource as BindingSource;
            bs?.EndEdit();
        }
    }
}
```

Alternatively, `RowValidated` can be used to commit a new row. But for recalculation when entering the second cell, `CellEndEdit` is the answer.

parts:
You need to force the “new‐row” in the RadGridView to actually commit itself into your BindingSource before you try to read/write the rest of the columns. The simplest pattern is:

1. Handle `CellEndEdit` (or `CellValueChanged`) for the columns you care about.  
2. In your handler, call `EndEdit` on both the grid **and** its binding source.  
3. Now the new object has been created in the data‐source, so you can do your calculations and write back into that object (or into other grid cells).

Here’s a drop-in example:

```csharp
// after you DataBind your RadGridView…
var bs = (BindingSource)radGridView1.MasterTemplate.DataSource;

// 1) subscribe once
radGridView1.CellEndEdit += RadGridView1_CellEndEdit;

private void RadGridView1_CellEndEdit(object sender, GridViewCellEventArgs e)
{
    // only trigger on the second column (or whatever column you choose)
    if (e.Column.Name != "Column2") return;

    // 2) commit the just‐edited cell into its row
    radGridView1.EndEdit();

    // 3) commit the row into the underlying list
    bs.EndEdit();

    // 4) grab the newly created/edited row
    var row = e.Row as GridViewDataRowInfo;
    if (row == null) return;

    // 5) now you can calculate the rest of the fields
    //    for example if Column1 & Column2 are ints:
    var a = Convert.ToInt32(row.Cells["Column1"].Value);
    var b = Convert.ToInt32(row.Cells["Column2"].Value);
    var sum = a + b;

    // 6) write it back into the grid (and binding source)
    row.Cells["CalculatedColumn"].Value = sum;
    bs.EndEdit(); // make sure the DTO in your list sees the new value
}
```

**Why this works**  
- `radGridView1.EndEdit()` closes out the in-grid editor so the grid’s row actually holds the new value.  
- `bs.EndEdit()` tells the `BindingSource` (your `List<YourDto>`) “yes, take whatever you’ve got in the current row and apply it to your underlying object (including inserting a brand-new one if this was the ‘add new’ row).”  
- Once that’s done, your DTO is in place and you can safely read/write other properties (and they’ll show up in your grid and your list).  

If you need the calculation to fire as soon as the user _leaves_ the row altogether, you can also do the same pattern in the `RowValidated` event instead of `CellEndEdit`. But for “after column 2” this is usually the most responsive.

