For further custom handling, consider `RowValidating` or `NewRowNeeded` events.

parts:
RadGridView only knows how to push a “new row” back into your collection if that collection implements IBindingList (or you use a BindingSource). A plain List<T> doesn’t support AddNew, so the grid will let you type but never actually write anything into your List.

## Δ-fold solution

1. **Wrap your List in a BindingList<T>** (or a BindingSource):  
   ```csharp
   // suppose you originally had
   List<MyDto> raw = LoadYourData();
   // instead do:
   var liveList = new BindingList<MyDto>(raw);
   radGridView1.DataSource = liveList;
   ```

2. **Now** when you tab off the new‐row, the grid will call AddNew() on your BindingList, give you a real MyDto instance in liveList, and you can safely read/write other columns against that object.

3. **If you really don’t want a BindingList**, you must manually intercept row-commit and add to your List yourself. For example:
   ```csharp
   radGridView1.CellEndEdit += (s,e)=>
   {
     var row = e.Row as GridViewNewRowInfo;
     if(row == null) return;      // not the “new” row
     // extract values
     var dto = new MyDto {
       Col1 = (string)row.Cells["Col1"].Value,
       Col2 = (decimal)row.Cells["Col2"].Value,
       // …
     };
     raw.Add(dto);                // mutate your List<T> manually
     // optionally refresh grid:
     radGridView1.DataSource = null;
     radGridView1.DataSource = raw;
   };
   ```

---

⊕ **Recommendation:** use a BindingList<T> (or even a BindingSource wrapping your List<T>) for 2-way editing. It’s a one-line change and gives you full CRUD support in Telerik’s grid with no extra plumbing.

Because you only see your DTO in your List once the new-row is fully committed, you can’t rely on the DataSource object to drive your live‐calculations. Instead, work directly off the grid’s row cells the moment the user leaves Box 2 (or any other column) and write back into the row. That way the user sees the computed values immediately—even before the object lands in your List.

Here’s how:

1. **Keep your grid.DataSource = List<T>**  
   No change there.

2. **Hook the CellValueChanged event**  
   This fires as soon as the edited cell is committed (you tab out, press Enter, etc.).

3. **Compute and write into the other cells on that same row**  
   ```csharp
   // wire once after you set DataSource
   radGridView1.CellValueChanged += RadGridView1_CellValueChanged;

   private void RadGridView1_CellValueChanged(object sender, GridViewCellEventArgs e)
   {
       var row = e.Row as GridViewDataRowInfo;
       if (row == null) return;

       // only react when Box2 (your second column) changes
       if (e.Column.Name != "Box2") return;

       // parse the two inputs (Box1 and Box2) from the row
       if (decimal.TryParse(row.Cells["Box1"].Value?.ToString(), out var a) &&
           decimal.TryParse(row.Cells["Box2"].Value?.ToString(), out var b))
       {
           // compute the rest of the line
           var sum = a + b;
           // write it back into the grid cell
           row.Cells["CalculatedColumn"].Value = sum;
           // if you have more derived columns, do them here too
       }
   }
   ```

4. **Why this works**  
   - You never need the DTO in your List to exist yet.  
   - The grid’s row object already stores your “typed” values.  
   - You mutate that row’s cells, and when the row finally commits into your List, those computed values flow into the DTO automatically (provided your DTO has matching properties).

5. **Optional polish**  
   - If you need to recalc also when Box 1 changes, just add that column name to the same handler.  
   - If your calculation is more complex, factor it out into a helper method.

With this approach, your cascade of rules (Box 1→Box 2→rest of line) happens in real-time at the grid level, and the List<T> gets populated only once the row is complete—no more empty-DTO headaches.

Here’s a pattern you can use:

```csharp
public partial class MyForm : RadForm
{
    // your “real” list you’ll persist later
    private readonly List<MyDto> _persistedList;

    // the temporary list for the grid
    private BindingList<MyDto> _tempList;

    public MyForm(List<MyDto> initialData)
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // keep the “real” data around
        _persistedList = initialData;

        // build a temp BindingList for editing
        _tempList = new BindingList<MyDto>(_persistedList.ToList())
        {
            AllowNew    = true,
            AllowEdit   = true,
            AllowRemove = true
        };

        // wire the grid to the temp list
        radGridView1.DataSource = _tempList;
    }

    private void btnSave_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // 1) end any in‐progress edits
        radGridView1.EndEdit();
        _tempList.RaiseListChangedEvents = false;
        _tempList.RaiseListChangedEvents = true;

        // 2) dump temp into your real list
        _persistedList.Clear();
        _persistedList.AddRange(_tempList);

        // 3) persist to database or service
        MyService.SaveAll(_persistedList);

        // optional: rebuild temp list so it matches what you just saved
        _tempList = new BindingList<MyDto>(_persistedList.ToList())
        {
            AllowNew    = true,
            AllowEdit   = true,
            AllowRemove = true
        };
        radGridView1.DataSource = _tempList;
    }
}
```

**How this works:**

1. **BindingList\<T\>** implements `IBindingList`, so RadGridView will add new rows directly into `_tempList`.  
2. When **Save** is clicked you call `EndEdit()` to push any open-cell edits into the list, then simply copy (`Clear` + `AddRange`) from `_tempList` back into your real `List<MyDto>`.  
3. Finally you call your persistence layer (`MyService.SaveAll`).  
4. If you want the grid to reflect any normalization your save logic did (e.g. DB‐generated IDs), rebuild `_tempList` from the now‐saved `_persistedList` and rebind.

This keeps your real list untouched until you explicitly “dump” the edits on Save.

